WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:39.540
So let’s take a look at the structure of
paradise as it’s presented in Genesis.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.000
So the first aspect of the initial paradaisal
state is un-selfconsciousness.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.739
Now if you look and factor analytic studies
of human personality you note that self-consciousness

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:56.390
although it is arguably our greatest gift
also loads almost entirely on the factor that

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.030
defines negative affect.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:03.649
And you might also notice that when you say,
I became self-conscious you generally put

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:07.990
a negative cast on that in that I was talking
before a group of people and suddenly I was

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.900
seized by self-consciousness.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.580
And as a consequence of that I was flooded
by negative emotion and was fundamentally

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.580
immobilized.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:22.170
So it’s a very paradoxical, a very paradoxical
state of being, our highest rational gift

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:29.140
say, and the only on that clearly distinguishes
us from animals is also that which, when manifested,

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.119
makes us almost unbearable anxious

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:36.270
The initial paradaisal state when Adam and
Eve first walked in the Garden of Eden is

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.829
characterized by an animal-like unselfconsciousness.

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:45.960
Adam and Eve, whatever they are, are not clearly
segregated from the rest of the world.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.159
They have no idea, for example, of their own
nakedness.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.619
And if you think about what nakedness means,
you immediately understand that that’s also

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:57.689
a very profound, dramatic representation.

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:02.200
You know with children around the age of 3
or 4, many of them regardless of their mode

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.430
of upbringing, start to become very concerned
about privacy, say with regard to bodily functions

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:11.560
and also very concerned about ever showing
themselves without clothing

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:18.420
and it’s perfectly reasonable to presume
that that’s a consequence of their emergent

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.790
self-consciousness, an event that takes place
somewhere between the ages of 2 and 5 and

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.660
that’s a defining moment that makes them
segregatable, say from their mother.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:34.160
And so you also have images of Paradise that
flow through Western history that are characterized

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.810
by the image of the unconscious union between
the mother and child

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:46.480
which is an imagistic representation that
eradicates the tension of self-consciousness

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.290
both for the mother and for the child.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:55.209
So the notion that the child is living in
a paradaisal condition that is somehow lost

00:02:55.209 --> 00:03:00.319
as he or she approaches adulthood gives another
sort of symbolic layer to the notion of the

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:02.720
pre-selfconscious paradise.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.430
It’s also a place where order and chaos
are in perfect balance

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:14.930
and you know that because what paradise means
is para – around, daisa – a wall, while

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.129
Eden means delight or a place of delight.

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:23.940
Paradaisa, paradise is a walled garden, a
walled place of delight

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.610
and a garden is precisely that place where
the forces of nature or chaos and the forces

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.670
of culture are held in perfect balance, right?

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.080
That’s what a garden is.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.830
It’s nature given form by culture and it’s
a place that’s archetypically pleasant a

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:44.630
place where the intervention of human activity
has produced a kind of stability that transcends

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:50.689
that of nature because it’s a cultural construct
but also that transcends that of culture because

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:55.860
all of the plants and the other growing things
that constitute a garden are somehow transcendent

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:03.829
even though they’re under the cultivating
hand of culture and the individual.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:10.049
If you look at the manner in which the fall
story is represented, you can also see that

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:14.640
the place of craziest stability can be regarded
as a kind of paradise.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.049
So if you remember the story of Moses leading
his people through the desert, it’s clearly

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:22.640
the case that when the Israelites were in
the desert, even though they got away from

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.860
the tyranny it was easy to look back and say
well you know, tough as it was, the place

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.240
that we were before was much better than the
place we are now.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:37.181
So it’s perfectly reasonable and expectable
for people who are caught in a crisis to look

00:04:37.181 --> 00:04:43.030
back to the time prior to the dawn of that
crisis with longing even if the crisis that

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.900
they’re presently experiencing is a necessary
precondition for further development of personality.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.770
So the story that’s laid out in Genesis
has its structure something like this.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:57.710
Before we became self-conscious the world
was perfect.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.790
As a consequence of the rise of self-consciousness
we were thrown out of the Garden of Eden,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:07.190
out of paradise and destined to live the profane
existence that characterizes our present mode

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.460
of being where we’re subject to knowledge
of mortality and the possibility of Illness

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.620
and alienation from God.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.590
And wouldn’t it be ever so great if we could
only return to that condition of unself-consciousness

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.180
and make all our problems go away.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.470
And you see this kind of pathological paradaisal
reminiscence manifesting itself in the most

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:33.760
banal forms of conservatism which are always
projecting the ideal – the ideal path somewhere

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.250
back into the unattainable reaches of time.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:42.930
And also in those situations that obtain psychologically
when people are absolutely possessed by depression

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:50.310
and anxiety and wish for their consciousness
to come to an end if not metaphorically so

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:57.190
they desire to sleep, then actually so that
suicide is viewed as a kind of unconsciousness

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:02.889
whose paradaisal nature, the absence of all
opposition, is viewed as clearly preferable

00:06:02.889 --> 00:06:07.300
to the difficulties of actually maintaining
being.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:17.690
Eliade says, the idea of paradise once and
then paradise lost is not something unique

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:20.360
to Western or great Eastern societies.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:26.460
It’s a widespread motif, just as wide as
the flood motif.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:29.310
Regardless of where you go in the world you
find this notion.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:34.870
“When heaven had been abruptly separated
from the earth, that is when it had become

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:41.110
remote as in our days, when the tree or vine
connecting earth to heaven had been cut or

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:45.770
the mountain which used to touch the sky had
been flattened out then the paradaisal stage

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:51.720
was over and man entered into his present
fallen condition.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:57.389
In effect all myths of paradise show us primordial
man enjoying a beatitude, a spontaneity and

00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:02.169
freedom which he has unfortunately lost in
consequence of the fall.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:07.080
That is of what followed upon a mythical event
that caused the rupture between heaven and

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.080
earth.”

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:20.449
As I said, Eden is delight, a place of delight,
by terminological definition whereas paradise

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:23.069
is a walled garden.

00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:30.160
I want to show you what knowing that does
for analysis of the relationship between eastern

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:31.770
and western thought.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:38.330
So let me tell you quickly the story of the
Buddha and I’m going to represent it fundamentally

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:41.099
like this.

00:07:41.099 --> 00:07:46.270
Buddha starts his life in what’s essentially
a walled garden by all – by all reasonable

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.199
comparative analysis.

00:07:48.199 --> 00:07:55.160
And as a consequence of his emergent self-consciousness,
the unself-conscious child-like perfection

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:00.660
of that early state is permanently disrupted
so you have a situation where the greatest

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:05.479
redemption story of the East follows precisely
the same dramatical tract as the greatest

00:08:05.479 --> 00:08:07.539
creation story of the West.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:08.990
So this is the story.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:13.639
Buddha’s father is visited by an angel who
tells him that his son is going to grow up

00:08:13.639 --> 00:08:19.610
to be the greatest temporal profane ruler
the world has ever seen or a great spiritual

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:20.610
leader.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.980
And his father, being a pragmatic and conservative
man, decides that there’s no possible way

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.919
I’m going to allow my son to take the ambivalent
road of spiritual enlightenment.

00:08:30.919 --> 00:08:37.599
I’m going to allow him to fall completely
in love with the world so that he will remain

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:40.190
attached to this domain.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:47.360
So prior to Buddha’s birth, his father constructed
a great city with walls around it and inside

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:55.190
that city he removes all signs of pain, frustration
and disappointment, any sign of ugliness and

00:08:55.190 --> 00:09:00.440
age, the only people who are allowed to exist
within this city, are those who are in perfect

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.280
mental and physical health, who are paragons
of beauty and virtue.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:14.059
And the idea that lurks behind that archetypal
story is that when a father has a child his

00:09:14.059 --> 00:09:19.790
moral obligation is to shield the developing
consciousness of that child from contact with

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:26.090
any of the horrors of life that could provide
the child with an experience too traumatic

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.710
for that developing consciousness to apprehend.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:33.179
So because it’s an archetypal story it relates
to the development of all people, not just

00:09:33.179 --> 00:09:37.959
the redemptive saviour and that’s the motif
that the Buddhist story initially follows.

00:09:37.959 --> 00:09:44.390
A good father makes his father fall in love
with life by enticing that child into a direct

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.930
relationship with all that life has to offer.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:52.160
So Buddha grows up within this walled garden,
this unselfconscious paradise.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.819
But precisely because he’s being shielded
to this degree and allowed to mature, his

00:09:57.819 --> 00:10:03.490
consciousness continues to expand and the
world outside the boundaries that his parents

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:07.420
have established for him starts to attract
his attention.

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:13.020
Now we know already that the forbidden fruit,
right, the lure of what’s outside the walls

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.260
is something that human beings just can’t
keep their mangy little paws off, right?

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:22.540
We are absolutely uncontrollably curious and
the best way to make sure that we investigate

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.699
something is to lay down a structure that
says, whatever you do under whatever circumstances

00:10:27.699 --> 00:10:30.030
never look there, right?

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:34.640
And then the automatic systems that underlie
our orienting and that motivate our seeking

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.630
experience are constantly pulling our attention
precisely to that forbidden spot, compelling

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:43.150
us to investigate exactly that which has been
forbidden.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:50.010
So because Buddha is a consciousness developing
in a healthy manner he immediately becomes

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:54.770
curious about what lies beyond the limits
that have been established with him and he

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:57.760
makes a decision to go outside of paradise.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.170
right, which seems a particularly ridiculous
thing to do, given that in principle he had

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:05.020
everything he could possibly want inside the
walls.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:09.860
But then again we have the troublesome notion
of the original sin of Adam, right, which

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:17.200
is that if any of you were offered a forbidden
fruit again under circumstances mythologically

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:21.170
equivalent to those that obtained in the beginning,
you’d immediately reach your hand out and

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:26.040
take it because what we haven’t got for
human beings is always far more compelling

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:35.040
than what we have got
So Buddha goes outside the walls.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:39.459
But his father, who’s a good father although
somewhat conservative, decides he’s going

00:11:39.459 --> 00:11:43.940
to rig the game a little bit so he gets rid
of everybody that’s diseased or unhappy

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:48.300
or uncomfortable or ugly or old or anything
that could possibly disturb the Buddha.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:53.770
and he lines the streets with flower waving
women and puts petals on the road and sends

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.580
his son out in a gilded chariot.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:12:00.600
But the gods who are lurking around, right,
the trouble making gods who represent chaos

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:06.700
and disorder and the unknown decide to send
in front of Buddha a sick man who hobbles

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:12.850
unsteadily into view.
and Buddha asks his retainer precisely what

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:18.350
this phenomena represents and his retainer
says, well you know, human beings like you

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:24.260
since you’re human are subject to the deterioration
of these mere physical powers in an arbitrary

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:25.260
way.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:28.440
And this man is one person who’s been so
afflicted.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:34.370
And so Buddha is completely disenchanted by
his exploratory move out into the terrible

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:38.630
unknown and runs back into the castle walls
and shuts the door and is perfectly happy

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.220
to think of nothing for months.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:46.260
But then as his anxiety habituates and his
curiosity grows, he can’t stand the notion

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:49.520
of never going outside the walls again and
outside he goes again.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:55.179
And this time after his father prepares the
route ever so carefully the gods send inside

00:12:55.179 --> 00:12:57.690
an old man who hobbles into view.

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:03.510
And Buddha look at him in shock and horror
and says to his retainer, just precisely what’s

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:04.819
going on here?

00:13:04.819 --> 00:13:08.900
And his retainer says, well that’s an old
man.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.110
Everybody gets old and you’re going to get
old too and that’s the way of all humanity.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:18.910
And that’s the point at which Buddha’s
self-consciousness expands not to only include

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:25.190
the possibility of degeneration but to include
the temporal horizon that’s characteristic

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.190
of life.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:30.980
And he finds that so terribly shocking that
he runs back into the castle and shuts the

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.329
walls down and plays with his friends for
another six months until his anxiety, or maybe

00:13:34.329 --> 00:13:38.510
a year, until his anxiety finally habituates
and he goes out one final time.

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:44.900
And this time the gods send a funeral parade
for him and he sees his first dead body.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:49.280
And this such a terrible shock to him that
he can’t even go back to the castle.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:54.790
So his father prepares for him a great party
in the woods near the castle full of nude

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:58.880
dancing women who are perfectly willing to
flaunt themselves and to offer themselves

00:13:58.880 --> 00:13:59.880
to him.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:04.750
But Buddha is so absolutely and catastrophically
shocked by this notion of emergent death that

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:09.140
he can’t take any pleasure whatsoever in
what’s being offered to him and he leaves

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.370
the kingdom once and for all.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:15.110
And you think, well that’s exactly what
happens to you when you grow up, right?

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:19.459
If you’re reasonably well socialized and
properly looked after then your curiosity

00:14:19.459 --> 00:14:23.460
gets the better of you and you keep going
out into the world until what your parents

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:27.069
have established for you is no longer sufficient
for you.

00:14:27.069 --> 00:14:31.130
And as a consequence of that movement out
into the world, you find out all sorts of

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:36.669
things characteristic of your own life that
not only your parents can’t precisely explain

00:14:36.669 --> 00:14:42.420
to you but even the broader formal structures
of your culture have a very difficult time

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:43.420
handling.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:49.490
And when you finally do encounter such realities
and allow their effect on you to fully manifest

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:55.339
itself well then you’re finally independent
and you no longer can return home.

00:14:55.339 --> 00:14:59.850
But from that point forward you’re also
burdened as Adam is burdened when he loses

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:06.140
his paradaisal unself-consciousness with the
full revelation of what it means to be limited

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:07.350
and alive.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:11.070
So what happens to Buddha as a consequence
of this revelation?

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:17.460
He becomes an apprentice and the chronicles
of the Buddha’s adventure are careful to

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:24.760
say that he becomes the world’s most proficient
practitioner of Samkhya, which was a philosophical

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.260
precursor to yoga and then to yoga.

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:31.569
So he masters all the positions and the asanas
until he’s disciplined physically to an

00:15:31.569 --> 00:15:33.540
almost unlimited degree.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:38.429
And then he decides that he’ll adopt a stance
of world renunciation which is also something

00:15:38.429 --> 00:15:40.140
he’s remarkably good at.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:44.699
And he starves himself until the chroniclers
say he resembles nothing so much as a pile

00:15:44.699 --> 00:15:45.920
of dust.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:51.140
And then, having exhausted all the disciplinary
structures that his sophisticated culture

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:57.240
has to offer him but still not precisely finding
the answer that he’s looking for, he retreats

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:03.320
into the forest, place of the unknown, and
sits himself at the base of a tree.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.520
Underneath the tree he’s visited by visions
and temptations.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:09.660
The first vision is an essentially erotic
one.

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:16.309
Life itself tempts him back out of his self-conscious
state into the domain of pure physical pleasure,

00:16:16.309 --> 00:16:20.530
a perfectly reasonable temptation, right,
and one that’s powerful enough so that Hindu

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:27.160
philosophers say as their churches and cathedrals
are covered with erotic drawings, if you can’t

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:31.390
get past the erotic drawings into the church
that’s the domain that you should still

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:32.470
inhabit, right.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.660
In the dawning phases of life at least till
middle age that’s the appropriate mode of

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:42.020
being to be enticed and seduced by the physical
pleasures that life has to offer.

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:47.870
But in the final analysis those are not sufficient
to solve the problem of emergent self-consciousness.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:55.280
And so the angel of death visits him and offers
him the opportunity to exist permanently in

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:56.360
a state of nirvana.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:17:02.020
A very, very interesting twist on the story
because you have to wonder, given the association

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:07.419
say between suicidelity and the notion of
paradise that exists underneath that, if what

00:17:07.419 --> 00:17:12.280
Buddha isn’t being offered by the angel
of death is in fact death and the cessation

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:17.579
of all the problems of being regardless he
rejects that, attains enlightenment briefly

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:22.600
and then decides to return to the world to
share what he’s discovered with all of suffering

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:23.600
humanity.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:29.760
The idea being that the Buddha who is the
awakened or enlightened one is capable of

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:36.440
attaining a transcendent state but also knows
fully that because human beings have a shared

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:42.550
social aspect it is not possible for any one
person to attain redemption until all people

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:48.490
attain redemption, the reason being that it’s
very difficult to be transcendent and enlightened

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:56.800
when you see someone who’s sick lying in
a ditch.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:06.250
So then we shift from that back to Genesis
and the tempted fall of man and read the third

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:07.250
chapter.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:10.410
And they were both naked, the man and his
wife and were not ashamed.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.720
Well what does that mean?

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:15.950
Well, Freud pointed out that one of human
being’s most common nightmares is to be

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.370
stripped of clothing in front of a crowd.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.880
Now why would that be precisely?

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:26.300
Well your naked self is the most vulnerable
aspect of you, right?

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:28.650
We’re all clothed and for good reason.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:32.140
Partly that’s protection from the terrible
natural world, right?

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:38.110
But it also offers us the possibility of placing
a barrier between ourselves, our vulnerable

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:42.610
selves and the searching and critical gaze
of the community, right?

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:47.200
Because not only are we vulnerable to the
rigours of nature, we’re also vulnerable

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:51.140
to the depredations and criticisms of society.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:56.550
And the notion that a man and a woman could
exist naked and not know it is a clear – is

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:03.780
a clear finger pointing in the direction of
a story that says these people were not conscious

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:07.870
or if conscious they certainly were not self-conscious.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:09.890
And how does the story develop?

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:14.820
"The serpent was more subtle than any beast
of the field which the Lord God had made.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.560
And he said unto the woman: yea, hath God
said you shall not eat of every tree in the

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:20.560
Garden.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:24.200
And the woman said unto the serpent, well
we may eat of the fruit of the trees of the

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:29.940
Garden but of the fruit of the tree which
is in the centre of the Garden God has said

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:33.690
you should not eat of it, neither shall you
touch it lest ye die.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:40.890
And the serpent said to the woman: you won’t
die for God knows that in the day you eat

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:47.130
thereof then your eyes shall be opened, right,
a clear pointing to the notion of an awakening

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:53.330
and an illumination-- And ye shall be like
gods knowing good and evil,"

00:19:53.330 --> 00:20:00.000
right, which attributes to humanity, a dawning
sense of morality explicit morality, the faculty

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.650
for comprehension that we do not share with
any other animal.

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:07.480
"And when the woman saw that the tree was
good for food and that it was pleasant to

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:11.680
the eyes and a tree to be desired to make
one wise, she took of the fruit thereof and

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:16.470
did eat and gave also unto her husband with
her and he did eat.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:19.270
"
Women, trouble.

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:21.590
What’s the tree?

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:23.630
Well we talked about this a little bit before.

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:29.470
So you say, well if you look at the structure
of experience from this particular perspective

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:33.220
and you think about that in a horizontal plane,
vertical plane, sorry, with the layers one

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:38.020
on top of another, then you can imagine the
tree as the thing that unites these three

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:39.020
layers.

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:43.770
The tree is the domain that unites chaos and
order and the individual, the structure running

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:45.260
up through the middle of it.

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:49.680
And you see a representation of that interestingly
enough, from Norse mythology.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:55.540
This is the Yggdrasill the axis mundi the
tree of the gods, the tree that stands in

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:58.280
the middle of the Norse paradise.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:01.400
And one of the things that’s very interesting
about this particular tree is that if you

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:06.570
look at its roots the roots are covered with
snakes and serpents and underneath the snakes

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.220
and serpents is water.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:14.980
And so that you see that the tree that stands
at the centre of the world is rooted in chaos

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:21.140
fundamentally, is rooted in whatever it is
that constitutes the pre-cosmogonic matrix

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:22.270
of being.

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:30.680
And then the central aspects of this domain
are nicely laid out as the domain of territoriality,

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:35.140
the ends of the borders that the individual
understands and the habitual territory that

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:41.020
he inhabits and then the tree in the centre
represents whatever it is that’s central

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:44.440
to this – to our mode of being.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:50.190
And so let’s take a look at that in some
detail and flesh it out symbolically.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:57.540
We find Eliade is saying the tree that stands
hypothetically at the centre of the world

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:03.020
is precisely that structure that shamans climb
when they make their transition from normal

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:06.400
mode of earthly being into their transcendent
mode of being.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:11.320
So Eliade says the symbolism of the ascension
into heaven by means of a tree is clearly

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:15.410
illustrated by the ceremony of initiation
of a Buryat shaman.

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:19.750
The candidate climbs up a post in the middle
of his yurt, his tent, reaches the summit

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:21.770
and goes out by the smoke hole.

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:25.990
But we know that this opening made to let
out the smoke is likened to the hole made

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:28.490
by the pole star in the bulk of heaven.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:33.530
So you can imagine there’s a conceptualization
of the world as centred around a particular

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:39.860
axis and that the tent is regarded as, at
least transitorily as a symbolic equivalent

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:43.050
of the cosmological structure.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:46.730
Among other peoples the tent hole is called
the pillar of the sky and is compared to the

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:52.070
pole star around which the world rotates,
at least from the visual perspective and is

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:53.930
named elsewhere the nail of the sky.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:58.230
Thus, the ritual post set up in the middle
of the yurt is an image of the cosmic tree

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:02.260
which is found at the centre of the world
with the pole star shining directly above

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:03.260
it.

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:06.520
By ascending it, the candidate enters into
heaven.

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:10.370
That is why as soon as he comes out of the
smoke hole of the tent he gives a loud cry

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:19.460
invoking the help of the gods Up there he
finds himself in their presence.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:26.480
The tree is an absolutely archaic symbol and
it seems to me most likely that it represents

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:28.450
the structure of the nervous system.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:33.750
I think a structure that’s rooted not so
much in the spinal, sensory motor structures

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:40.420
but deeply in the autonomic structures, stretching
down into the centre of the body and planting

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:45.980
the mind firmly in its material substrate
so that the autonomic system and its projections

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:51.260
up into the amigdylla and limbic system and
then up into the cortex constitute the interface

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:56.360
between the spiritual domain that our psyche
inhabits and the material domain that constitutes

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:57.490
our body.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:02.330
The tree at the centre of our being.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:03.330
What does the tree do?

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:04.900
Well it bears fruit.

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:06.330
Well what sort of fruit?

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:11.630
Well there are multiple medieval representations
that are quite peculiar showing Christ, for

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.500
example, as the fruit of the tree of the knowledge
of good and evil.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:16.650
The fruit of the tree of life.

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:17.960
And what does that mean?

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:23.350
It means that conceptualizations like the
hero are the products of whatever it is that

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:25.610
this tree represents.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:28.110
The tree is something that produces fruit.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:31.390
Fruit is also something that can be ingested.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:42.190
And as Eric Newman points out wherever liquor,
fruit, herbs, etc. appear as the vehicles

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:47.130
of life and immortality, including the water
and bread of life, the sacrament of the host

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:52.230
and every form of food cult down to the present
day we have an ancient mode of human expression

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:53.350
before us.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:59.280
So imagine the idea of the – of the Piagetian
idea of assimilation and then accommodation

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:05.340
and then understand its assimilative, nutritive,
underlying metaphorical nature.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:11.010
The idea being that there’s a tight analogy
between ingesting something material and undergoing

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.770
a transformation of energy.

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:18.200
Attendant upon that, right, which is what
happens when you eat and ingesting a piece

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:23.580
of information which offers you a new mode
of doing things so you can say well people

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.200
will trade work for information.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:29.450
They will trade food for information.

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:33.950
There must be a kind of equivalence between
work and information and food and information

00:25:33.950 --> 00:25:36.320
because otherwise the trade wouldn’t make
sense.

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:42.330
And then you realize that if you’re informed
you can undertake transformations of yourself

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:46.900
and the material world in a much more efficient
manner because that’s what being informed

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:47.900
means.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:52.640
And that means that being informed, acquiring
some information and eating something are

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:56.980
all tied up in a complex way into the same
metaphorical structure.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:02.140
Eat something forbidden, transform as a consequence.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:07.040
Conscious realization is acted out in the
elementary scheme of nutritive assimilation

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:11.650
and a ritual act of concrete eating is the
first assimilation known to man.

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:12.650
Conscious realization is acted out through
ritual by an act of eating.

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:13.650
Since the act of eating represents a very
familiar example of assimilation.

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:19.080
The assimilation and ingestion of the content,
the eaten food, produces an inner change.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:23.050
Transformation of the body cell through food
intake is the most elementary of animal changes

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:25.680
experienced by man.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:30.690
How a weary, enfeebled and famished man can
be turned into an alert, strong and satisfied

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:35.880
being or a man perishing of thirst can be
refreshed or even transformed by an intoxicating

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:36.880
drink.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:42.180
This is and must remain a fundamental experience
so long a man shall exist.

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:49.800
Eric Newman’s point being that our psychological
experience of the capacity of psychological

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:56.170
transformation through eating is a metaphor
waiting to be applied to the equivalent experience

00:26:56.170 --> 00:27:00.930
that we obtain, the equivalent excitement
and sense of transformative possibility that

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:07.510
we acquire as a consequence of coming across
some new and truly valuable piece of information.

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:12.510
So you have the idea that the tree that stands
at the centre of the world, the individual

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:15.720
mode of being, is something that bears fruit.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:20.630
And the ingestion of that fruit, that idea
say or that piece of information is something

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:23.540
that can produce a permanent transformation.

